To run the same code inline on multiple named variables in C++, you can use a lambda function or a function pointer. Here's a concise way to do it:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void process(int value) {
    std::cout << "Processed: " << value << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20, z = 30;

    // Using a lambda function
    auto processAll = [](int value) { process(value); };
    processAll(x);
    processAll(y);
    processAll(z);

    // Using a function pointer
    void (*processPtr)(int) = process;
    processPtr(x);
    processPtr(y);
    processPtr(z);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the lambda function `processAll` and the function pointer `processPtr` are used to call the `process` function with each of the variables `x`, `y`, and `z`. This approach allows you to run the same code on multiple variables in a concise manner.

